  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nSunday through Tuesday, the center of an an upper level ridge shall \nhover near the Four Corners region. The ridge axis will slowly \nexpand and drift eastward through the middle of the week before \nweakening and eventually setting up over the southeastern US. Until \nthen, the forecast remains hot and dry for most of our region \nthrough midweek. Afternoon highs tick up a degree or two over each \nprogressive day Sunday through Wednesday, generally ranging within \nthe upper 90s to low 100s. The hottest locations will be near and \nalong the river valleys, especially the Rio Grande, where highs are \nforecast to reach just shy of 110 degrees Wednesday. Most areas are \nexpected to remain dry during the first half of the week. However, \nisolated showers and storms may develop each afternoon in the \nvicinity of the higher terrain, primarily the Davis Mountains \nthrough Tuesday. With the center of the ridge shifting to our east \nduring the latter half of the week, better rain chances begin to \nenter the forecast Wednesday. Wednesday evening currently features \nrain chances of 10-30% over the western half of our region. Thursday \nafternoon, isolated to scattered showers and storms will be possible \nwest of the Pecos River, expanding in coverage eastward Thursday \nevening. Temperatures also begin a slight downward trend as we \nhead toward the end of the week. Overnight lows through the period\nshall cool to the mid 60s to mid 70s.",
